<reference_puzzle index="1" source="eval" puzzle_id="de809cff" lines="86-103">
<description>
Turn black dents in two colored regions into marked opposite-color patches.

Input structure:
- Black background with two large foreground colors.
- The large regions contain small black dents and a few one-cell color specks
  or protrusions along region boundaries.

Rule:
- Locally clean the two foreground regions: isolated specks become black, and
  one-cell protrusions surrounded by the other region take that region's color.
- Each original black dent whose cleaned 3x3 neighborhood is mostly one
  foreground color becomes the center of a 3x3 patch in the opposite color.
- The dent center itself is recolored teal (8).

Key insight: The teal pixels mark the original black defects; the surrounding
3x3 patch uses the other foreground color, after first removing boundary noise.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="4df5b0ae" lines="27-41">
<description>
Pack the colored sprites along the bottom edge from smallest to largest.

Input structure
The grid has a dominant background color and several monochrome 4-connected
foreground sprites; a full-grid one-cell frame may surround the scene.

Rule
Remove any full-grid rectangular frame, sort the remaining sprites by pixel
area from smallest to largest, and paste their bounding-box sprites left to
right with each sprite bottom-aligned.

Key insight
The original locations do not determine the final slots; each sprite's area
gives the packing order, while its shape and color are preserved.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="eval" puzzle_id="abc82100" lines="70-86">
<description>
Use local source-target-pattern examples to replace matching source pixels.

Input structure:
- Black background with isolated colored pixels and teal (8) pattern components.
- A rule example is a 4-neighbor colored pair source->target whose continued
  direction reaches an anchor touching a teal pattern component.

Rule:
- For each rule example, copy that teal component onto every other pixel of
  source.color, recoloring the copied component to target.color.
- Translation maps the rule anchor onto the source pixel being replaced.
- Remove teal components and all pixels used by rule examples.

Key insight: The two adjacent colored pixels define the color substitution and
the anchor point; the nearby teal component is the stencil that gets recolored.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="11dc524f" lines="25-40">
<description>
Slide the non-square sprite to the square anchor and replace the anchor with the sprite's mirror image.

Input structure:
- The background is the most common color.
- There is one 8-connected non-background sprite that is not a solid 2x2 square.
- There is one solid 2x2 square anchor in another non-background color.

Rule:
- Move the sprite straight toward the anchor along their unique cardinal separation until it touches the anchor's near edge.
- Reflect the moved sprite across that near edge and draw the reflected copy in the anchor color.
- Clear the original locations; the output keeps only the moved sprite and reflected anchor-color copy.

Key insight:
- The 2x2 square marks the mirror line: its side facing the sprite becomes the contact edge for a two-color reflected pair.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="c6141b15" lines="84-95">
<description>
Swap a connector segment with repeated marker sprites by connecting the marker anchors and stamping the sprite at the segment endpoints.

Input structure
The grid has one background color and exactly two foreground colors. One foreground color forms a single straight 8-connected segment. The other foreground color forms two or more identical centered sprites.

Rule
Build a background canvas of the same size. Draw straight connector-color segments between every pair of original sprite anchors. Then stamp the marker sprite, using its centered anchor, onto the two endpoints of the original connector segment.

Key insight
The original segment endpoints are the destinations for the repeated sprite, while the original sprite centers become the vertices of the new connector-color complete graph.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="1be83260" lines="41-58">
<description>
Decode the colored slot map inside the marked motif and rebuild the motif lattice with those colors.

Input structure:
- A blank background surrounds a rectangular lattice of identical motifs.
- The motif rows and columns are separated by one background line.
- One motif has colored marker pixels in its internal odd-row/odd-column slots.

Rule:
- The motif size is 2R+1 by 2C+1, where R by C is the motif lattice size.
- Read the marked motif's odd internal slots as the color map for the R by C output motifs.
- Slots that still contain the repeated motif color map to that repeated motif color.
- Rebuild the compact lattice on a canvas filled with the repeated motif color,
  painting each motif mask with its decoded color.

Key insight:
- The holes and filled odd slots inside one motif are a miniature legend for recoloring the entire repeated motif lattice.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="a6f40cea" lines="74-88">
<description>
Complete partial paths inside a framed rectangle by projecting external color traces inward.

Input structure:
- bg, a 1px rectangular frame (border color) with uniform interior fill.
- Colored components outside the frame touching its edges.

Rule:
- Output = the frame's interior, filled with the fill color.
- Map each outside component's frame-touch points into interior coordinates.
- Monochrome components become solid Manhattan stripes between mapped endpoints.
- Two-color components become alternating-color Manhattan paths.

Key insight: Endpoint colors of two-color traces anchor the alternating phase deterministically.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="458e3a53" lines="19-32">
<description>
Extract the compact table of solid cells hidden inside a separator lattice.

Input structure
- The grid has full-width and full-height bars of one separator color, dividing it into rectangular cells.
- Most cells contain a repeating multicolor pattern, while a contiguous rectangle of cells is solid monochrome.

Rule
- Split the input into lattice cells using the full separator rows and columns.
- Keep the cells whose full interior is one color, crop to their occupied rectangle, and output those colors.

Key insight
- The periodic pattern is noise; only completely solid lattice cells encode the reduced output grid.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="eval" puzzle_id="800d221b" lines="7-22">
<description>
Recolor a path-network by adjacent seed regions.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Path color P forms a thin orthogonal network with a solid square hub (NxN).
- Seed colors appear in colored regions (non-bg, non-P) adjacent to path branches.

Rule:
- Infer P as the non-bg color whose largest component is most network-like.
- Find the largest solid k×k all-P square (the hub); exclude it from recoloring.
- Recolor each path branch to the seed-region color it touches most.
- Recolor the hub center pixel to the seed whose branches touch the hub most.

Key insight: The hub stays uncolored; branches inherit color from adjacent seed regions by adjacency count.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="2b9ef948" lines="51-65">
<description>
Move the yellow 3x3 frame by the displacement encoded by the gray path, then draw its diagonal yellow corner rays.

Input structure:
- The background is black.
- One 3x3 yellow frame has a non-yellow control-color center.
- A second control-color pixel is connected through gray path pixels to a lone yellow endpoint.

Rule:
- Use the vector from the second control-color pixel to the lone yellow endpoint as the frame's translation.
- Fill the whole output with the control color.
- Draw the translated yellow frame and extend yellow diagonal rays outward from its four corners to the grid edge.

Key insight: The gray path's turns are irrelevant; its two colored endpoints encode the net displacement to apply to the frame.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="eval" puzzle_id="3e6067c3" lines="7-20">
<description>
Connect framed squares via colored bridges following a control-pixel sequence.

Input structure:
- bg = background. Border color forms equal-sized framed squares with unique inner fill colors.
- Control row below squares: colored pixels read left-to-right encode visitation order.

Rule:
- Start at the square matching first control color c0.
- For each consecutive pair (ci, ci+1), find the ci+1-colored square aligned in the same row or column band, preferring nearest.
- Draw a bridge colored ci between the pair, spanning the gap with inner-square thickness.

Key insight: Row/column-band alignment plus proximity resolves ambiguity when inner colors repeat.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="train" puzzle_id="973e499e" lines="7-22">
<description>
Expand each input pixel into a miniature same-color mask of the whole grid.

Input structure
- The input is a non-empty two-dimensional grid of foreground colors.
- Black is reserved for the empty/background pixels introduced in the expanded output.
- The output has one input-shaped block for each input pixel.

Rule
- For every input pixel with color c, replace that pixel by a block the same shape as the input.
- Inside the block, write c exactly where the original input also has color c.
- Write black everywhere else in the block.

Key insight
Each pixel selects the global mask of its own color; the result is the blockwise self-mask of the grid.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="train" puzzle_id="ff2825db" lines="18-36">
<description>
Select the majority interior marker color, then redraw the framed board as that color's frame plus its bounding-box outline.

Input structure:
- The top row is a copied palette made from adjacent equal color pairs.
- Below it is a one-cell-thick rectangular frame around a black interior.
- Non-black, non-frame interior pixels are marker colors; one marker color has
  the unique highest count.

Rule:
- Keep the palette row unchanged.
- Pick the uniquely most frequent interior marker color.
- Clear the lower board to black, recolor its outer frame with the picked color,
  and draw the picked color's interior bounding-box border.

Key insight:
- The noisy marker cloud is not preserved as pixels; only the majority marker
  color and its spatial extent define the output.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

